   "notes": [
    "Actual results for calendar year 2025, as the city reported them in its annual report to the Washington State Auditor. Not a budget.",
    "Public schools are run by separate school districts, so they are not included. Residents also pay Jefferson County.",
    "Spending is reported by function (BARS account) for every fund, utilities included; capital expenditures count under their function when paid.",
    "Internal service funds (equipment rental, self-insurance, information technology and the like; $6,604,900 of their revenue and spending) are left out, since the departments they serve report that spending.",
    "Borrowing, transfers between funds, interfund loans, refunded debt and depreciation are left out.",
    "General government, courts, legal services, planning, economic development, housing and conservation count as overhead.",
    "Political money is not scored: no candidates for mayor and council of City of Port Townsend reported business, union or PAC contributions to the Public Disclosure Commission since 2023.",
    "Transparency practices have not been checked yet, so they are not scored.",
    "Cameras: 9 license-plate cameras are mapped inside City of Port Townsend's borders in OpenStreetMap (0 made by Flock Safety), not counting cameras tagged as run by stores, malls, schools or state and regional agencies. Flock cameras count as a red flag whoever runs them. Other brands count only when the government is the recorded operator, since many have no operator recorded.",
    "Red flags: surveillance programs come from the EFF Atlas of Surveillance, a public database of news reports and public records, searched on 2026-10-18. A town or county with none listed may still have programs nobody has reported. Data center deals and corporate tax breaks have not been checked for Washington yet. Most records carry no dollar amount, so each documented program costs a quarter of the red-flag points instead of being counted as spending.",
